#!/bin/sh
# Deletes nothing. On purpose.
#
# This is issue #85's "uninstall behavior does not unexpectedly delete
# retained backup data" criterion, and the safest implementation of it is
# an empty one. Three separate things would have to be deleted for that
# criterion to fail, and none of them is this script's business:
#
#   * The backup data itself. It lives in the DSM shared folder the
#     data-share resource worker created, which Synology documents as
#     never removed on uninstall "since it might delete the user's
#     personal data as well". Removing it here would defeat exactly the
#     protection that worker exists to provide.
#   * The SQLite lifecycle journal and the local-auth administrator
#     record, both under /var/packages/<pkg>/var, which the package FHS
#     documents as surviving an uninstall.
#   * The configuration under /var/packages/<pkg>/etc, likewise.
#
# What DSM removes on its own is the target directory, which holds only
# the two release binaries and the DSM UI files - all of them replaceable
# by reinstalling. So there is nothing left for this script to clean up,
# and every deletion it could add would be a deletion of somebody's data.
exit 0
